      {
        "id": "prose",
        "role": "prose",
        "text": "A page begins to speak before we read its first sentence. The width of a column, the space between lines, and the quiet around a heading tell us how to approach it. Those relationships change with the words, the typeface, and the screen. A useful tool should make that change visible while leaving room for judgment.\n\nConsider a heading enlarged by a few points. It may cross a wrapping threshold, add a line, and pull the paragraph below it down the page. The right response may involve a wider measure, a smaller gap, or simply accepting the new rhythm. No single ratio can settle that choice.",
